WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0029: the three hands of a clock.
A junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.267
Two parts: how many meetings a day,
then the general formula and the proof.

3
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:15.867
The clock has three hands, and all of them move smoothly.
Even the second hand glides, it does not jump.

4
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:20.667
The second hand makes a turn in a minute,
the minute hand in an hour, the hour hand in 12 hours.

5
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:25.400
A meeting: a moment when two hands or more
point in exactly the same direction.

6
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.400
Here the second and minute hands meet,
at 0:01:01.02. We will see why exactly there.

7
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.300
Part A: how many times a day
do at least two hands meet?

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.500
The day: from 00:00:00 to 23:59:59, inclusive.
The midnight at its end belongs to the next day.

9
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.033
And a moment when all three meet
is counted only once.

10
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:50.433
The idea: count each pair of hands on its own,
then fix what was counted more than once.

11
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:56.133
Step 1: a day has 86,400 seconds.
How many turns does each hand make in it?

12
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:00.733
The second hand: 86,400 divided by 60,
that is 1,440 turns a day.

13
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:04.633
The minute hand makes 24 turns,
and the hour hand only 2.

14
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:10.533
Step 2: in each pair, the fast hand laps the slow one,
like a fast runner on a track.

15
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:16.533
Every time it gains a whole turn on it,
they meet. Here, minutes and hours.

16
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:22.533
In 12 hours the minute hand makes 12 turns,
the hour hand one. It gains 11.

17
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:26.500
The meeting at 12:00 is already the 12th,
because 00:00 was counted too.

18
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.300
Over a day the gap between two hands grows
at a constant rate, from 0 to the difference in turns.

19
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:37.300
They meet every time the gap is a whole number.
The final gap comes only at the midnight that ends the day.

20
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.300
So for each pair, the number of meetings a day
is the difference between their numbers of turns.

21
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.467
Seconds and minutes: 1,440 minus 24,
that is 1,416 meetings.

22
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:50.067
Seconds and hours: 1,438.
Minutes and hours: 22.

23
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:55.900
In the page's box: a clock that jumps
from meeting to meeting. We start at 00:00.

24
00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.367
Only the seconds and minutes pair stays checked,
so the jumps are its own.

25
00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:06.500
The first meeting: 0:01:01.02, a little after a minute,
because the minute hand moved on meanwhile.

26
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.967
The next at 0:02:02.03,
again 61.02 seconds later.

27
00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:14.633
And 0:03:03.05. The gap is constant:
3,600 over 59, about 61.02 seconds.

28
00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:19.300
Now seconds and hours, from 00:00.
The hour hand almost stands still.

29
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:24.267
A meeting at 0:01:00.08: a minute and a bit,
because the hour hand moves a little.

30
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:29.433
Then 0:02:00.17 and 0:03:00.25:
every 43,200 over 719, about 60.08 seconds.

31
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:33.233
And the slow pair, minutes and hours.
Again from 00:00.

32
00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:38.367
The first meeting: 1:05:27.27, not 1:05.
The hour hand has already passed the 1.

33
00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:43.333
The second at 2:10:54.55. The gap is constant:
an hour, 5 minutes and 27.27 seconds.

34
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:47.333
The gap is constant because the angle gap grows steadily.
So the meetings are equally spaced.

35
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:52.800
Now all the pairs together. Over a day the sum
is 1,416 plus 1,438 plus 22: 2,876.

36
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.700
But that is the number of meetings of pairs,
not the number of moments.

37
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.500
The "next triple" button jumps to 12:00:
all three hands on the 12.

38
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.500
Such a moment is counted in all three pairs
instead of once. Two too many.

39
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.500
A day has two such moments, 00:00 and 12:00.
We subtract 2 for each.

40
00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.300
The sum of the pairs: 2,876.

41
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.833
The answer: 2,876 minus 2 times 2,
that is 2,872 meetings a day.

42
00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:22.633
Why only two moments? Every meeting of all three
is also a meeting of the minutes and hours.

43
00:03:22.633 --> 00:03:27.300
In 12 hours there are only 11 of those.
We check where the second hand is at each.

44
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.700
At meeting 1, minutes and hours are at tick 5.45,
and the second hand at tick 27.27. Not together.

45
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:38.200
So in every row, except 0:00:00 and 12:00:00.
Only there is the second hand on the same tick.

46
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
The trap: count 12 hours and double it.
Without the ends, 12 hours have 1,435 meetings.

47
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.033
Twice that is 2,870, and the moments
00:00 and 12:00 themselves are missing.

48
00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:51.033
With both of them: 2,872.
The same answer.

49
00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:56.933
Part B, a bonus: a formula for any two hands,
and a proof of when all three meet.

50
00:03:57.633 --> 00:04:02.367
Two hands, with turn times t_i < t_j.
How often do they meet?

51
00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:07.367
Measure angles in turns. A hand with turn time t_i
makes 1/t_i turns a second.

52
00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:11.600
Step 1: the fast hand moves away from the slow one
at the difference of their speeds.

53
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.067
On the graph: the gap in turns against time
is a straight line rising at a constant rate.

54
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:21.667
The dashed lines are the whole numbers.
Every crossing is a meeting.

55
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:26.767
Step 2: the crossings are equally spaced, T apart.
The gap grows by a whole turn every T.

56
00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:31.733
In one T the gap grows by one turn:
the rate times T equals 1.

57
00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:35.700
So T is the product of the turn times
divided by their difference.

58
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.700
Put in the clock: for seconds and minutes
it is 3,600 over 59, about 61.02 seconds.

59
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:46.300
For seconds and hours about 60.08 seconds, for minutes
and hours about 3,927.27. As in part A's box.

60
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:52.100
Step 3: how many meetings in an interval of length L
that starts at a meeting? It depends on the ends.

61
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.067
The meetings are at 0, T, 2T and so on.
Count how many fall in the interval.

62
00:04:57.067 --> 00:05:01.800
In a closed interval: every k from 0 to L/T,
that is L/T rounded down, plus 1.

63
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.200
An open interval drops both 0 and L,
a half-open one only L.

64
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.400
In the page's box: minutes and hours, with
an interval of 3T. Closed: 4 meetings.

65
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.533
Open: a hollow dot is not counted.
Both ends are out, and 2 meetings remain.

66
00:05:17.533 --> 00:05:21.200
Half-open: 0 in and L out,
so 3 meetings.

67
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.000
When L is 2.75T, the end L is not a meeting.
Half-open: still 3.

68
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.233
Then only the end 0 matters: closed
has 3, like half-open.

69
00:05:30.233 --> 00:05:33.367
And open has 2,
without the end 0.

70
00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:39.667
Over 12 hours, L/T is 708, 719 and 11:
exactly the differences in turns, as in part A.

71
00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:45.067
And over a day, half-open: 1,416, 1,438
and 22. The same numbers as in part A.

72
00:05:45.867 --> 00:05:49.867
Step 4: a meeting of all three is two
meetings of pairs at the same moment.

73
00:05:49.867 --> 00:05:54.600
Seconds and minutes at meeting a, minutes
and hours at meeting b, at the same time.

74
00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.600
Divide both sides by 3,600.

75
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.700
Multiply by 59 and by 11, and we get
an equation in whole numbers.

76
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:06.233
But 708 is 4 times 3 times 59,
and it has no common divisor with 11.

77
00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:09.633
So 11 divides b: b = 11c,
and a = 708c.

78
00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:13.600
And the time is 43,200 times c:
only multiples of 12 hours.

79
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.600
In a day these are c = 0 and c = 1,
that is 00:00 and 12:00 only.

80
00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.400
Step 5: a moment of two hands belongs to one pair,
a moment of three to all three pairs.

81
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.200
So we subtract twice the number
of meetings of all three.

82
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.000
Over a day: 1,416 plus 1,438 plus 22,
minus 2 times 2. Again 2,872.

83
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.000
And the open 12 hours have no meeting of three:
1,435, as in the check of part A.

84
00:06:37.798 --> 00:06:43.398
In short: each pair on its own, then subtract
the moments of three. 2,872 meetings a day.

85
00:06:43.398 --> 00:06:48.798
Try the clock and the graph in the boxes
on the page of question 0029 on the site.
